{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Independent exact-head review of b4f7d459aeafc433f16a6860019240f73f0af3b2 on request/github-observation-force, under review request bcac8129 and promise 31eddf27, co-signing both artifacts: cbc08214 at internal/connector and f162fc09 at cmd/gitseq-github, governed by my request 9a6f2a9c.\n\nThird head in this lane answering a request I wrote, so the same discipline: check the code against the condition text rather than against the fix I imagined. On this one I deliberately left the placement open, so the question I owed was whether the choice is right.\n\nTHE PLACEMENT IS RIGHT, AND IT IS THE ONE I ARGUED FOR WITHOUT MANDATING. Effectiveness rides on the Statement shape, exactly as authorship does, and the filter lives inside Fold as a second early continue before the source and external_id checks and before the first-writer record. Filtering in the adapter instead would have left the library wrong for its next caller; this way the library states its own admission rule and any caller inherits it. Four files, +52/-14, cut from current main c2b58f73.\n\nTHE ADAPTER DERIVES FORCE FROM DECISIONS AND NOTHING ELSE. observedStatements builds a map from projection.Decisions keyed by event, with the value decision.Verdict == workroom.Effective, and passes it through per statement. No body field is consulted, nothing is inferred from kind or shape, and the fold's own verdict vocabulary is the only source. That matters because the defect being closed was precisely the projection lending weight to records it had refused; reading the refusal from the same projection is the honest way to undo that.\n\nMISSING DECISIONS FAIL CLOSED, AND I PROVED IT RATHER THAN ARGUING IT FROM THE ZERO VALUE. This is the condition I cared about most, because a filter that silently admits the unknown is not a filter. The Go argument is easy — a map miss yields false — but the property depends on the whole path, so I wrote a throwaway probe at the exact head: a projection carrying the statement and an empty Decisions slice. The correspondence came back map[] and the observation stayed fresh. A statement with no verdict cannot suppress anything.\n\nBOTH NAMED MUTATIONS RE-RUN BY ME, AND ONE OF THEM BITES HARDER THAN CLAIMED. Removing the effectiveness guard from Fold fails TestIneffectiveConnectorStatementCannotSuppressTheGenuineObservation with \"ineffective connector statement suppressed the genuine observation\". Making the adapter mark every statement effective fails that same test and also fails TestTheCommandAdmitsOnlyEffectiveClausesCitingTheSelectedCharter, which admitted two clauses instead of one — so the effectiveness map is shared with clause admission and a mistake there is caught twice, by two tests written for different reasons. That second failure is not in the recorded evidence and is worth having on the record.\n\nCONDITION 3 HOLDS. First-writer-wins between two effective observations is unchanged: TestFoldKeepsTheFirstObservation still pins it, updated only to mark its fixtures effective, and the record at the bottom of the loop is untouched. The library remains pure and stateless; Fold gained a field on its input, not a dependency.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head with a clean worktree. gofmt -l over cmd, internal, host and spike: no output. git diff --check main...HEAD: clean. make vet: clean. make build: both binaries. go test ./... -count=1: 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL, internal/apphost has no test files.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are Application connector — internal/connector/github and cmd/gitseq-github — which docs/reference/architecture.md describes as applying Workroom charters and emitting Workroom observations, replaceable and outside the kernel. Both contracts preserved. The connector library learned that its input carries a verdict; it did not learn how verdicts are decided, which stays in internal/workroom where it belongs. No layer boundary moved and no signature outside this package changed. No contract change, so no architecture page update is required and none was made.\n\nSECURITY. The boundary here is force rather than identity: the previous head stopped a foreign author, this one stops the connector's own refused acts. Untrusted input: none newly admitted; the change only narrows. Failure modes: the unknown-verdict case falls closed, proven above, and the refused case falls closed, tested. Ordering: a refused statement can no longer occupy an external id ahead of a genuine one, which was the whole defect. Trust: effectiveness comes from the fold's decision, the same authority that already governs whether a record has force anywhere else, so no second notion of validity is introduced. No private state added. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. One field, one early continue, one map in the adapter; the diff is mostly test fixtures gaining Effective: true, which is the honest cost of adding a required field rather than a default.\n\nOne coverage observation, recorded rather than requested. When I removed the Fold guard, internal/connector/github's own suite stayed green — every library test sets Effective: true, so only the cmd-level test caught it. The condition asked for an adversarial test that fails when the filter is removed and that is satisfied, but the guard lives in the library while the test that defends it lives in the caller. A library-level case with Effective: false would protect the next caller directly. Not worth a new head; worth adding whenever that file is next open.\n\nMergeable. The head is pushed and cut from current main; both artifacts are live, ordinarily stale, and neither describes a superseded world; codex retired the unclaimed alternate review route before reissuing this one, so no competing verdict exists on the same head; and this approval names both artifacts, so a receipt may retire each path's predecessor.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cbc082141c760ab7c713edae42d70d78c5629134","head":"b4f7d459aeafc433f16a6860019240f73f0af3b2","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ac3403bdc4f71b6fd4080869b7ffb04f335a0ad7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e7f4300a665397b28f328be736b2531256ec3623, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f23514ba79cc373b0ffd8b4bc2cedf114b9bbdc7","verdict":"approved"}}